  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2019-08-19T15:49:50.193Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "It'd be nice if we had an in-game method of scaling our arms/legs on any avatar.\n\nIn order to get a model to really work for a fullbody user, it has to be edited in very specific ways.\n\nKung has a nice tutorial detailing the process:\nhttps://youtu.be/IqUVFuSD5a4?t=799\n\nThere's three major problems with this:\n\n1. Not everyone has access to editing every avatar they might want to use - public avatars for example.\n\n2. Even with detailed tutorials like this, it's not so simple for every user to simply learn how to take a model, edit it, and upload it.\n\n3. Even if you really know how to fix models for this, you need to go edit the model in blender, then import it to unity and set it up, then go into VR to actually test it with your fullbody trackers. If you're off by even a little bit, it's back to blender, and then you have to repeat a lot of steps, then test it again. It takes a lot of time to do these edits sometimes, and a lot of testing.\n\n----\n\nIt'd be much easier, and globally accessible, if we had a per-avatar setting that could allow us to re-proportionalize any avatar.\n\nI know some MMO character creators can do things like that, but dunno if it's possible in VRChat with unity. Nonetheless, it'd be useful.\n\nI'll also note that a rescale feature like this won't be totally nice for all model designs. For example, if you use a model with a pair of gloves or gauntlets, you may want to scale only the arm and not the hands or a part of a gauntlet that covers the arm past the wrist. For those, people will still have to edit things in blender if they want to avoid stretching things like that.",
